# tests/known_fail/u9_atomic.rxt -- U9, and pcrec DISAGREES WITH libpcre2 here.
#
# Read docs/dev/upstream_issues.md U9 first. On libpcre2 10.46 a possessive or
# atomic BOUNDED repeat `{m,n}+` of a GROUP, preceded by an item that consumed
# and CAN GIVE BACK, refuses to backtrack into that preceding item:
#
#     a?(?:b){0,4}+a   on "a"   libpcre2: NO MATCH   python + a hand
#     (a?)(?>(b){0,4})a on "a"  libpcre2: NO MATCH   derivation: (0,1)
#
# All three conjuncts are necessary, and the three CONTROLS that each drop one
# are live and green in tests/atomic_groups/possessive.rxt section 9:
# `a?b{0,4}+a` (a character item, not a group), `a?(?:b)*+a` (`*+`, not
# `{m,n}+`) and `x?(?:b){0,4}+a` (the prefix consumed nothing) all MATCH in
# libpcre2, in python and in pcrec.
#
# WHY THESE TWO CELLS ARE HERE RATHER THAN IN THE MODULE'S OWN DIRECTORY.
# D26 makes PCRE2 the SOURCE OF TRUTH for what a pattern matches, so libpcre2's
# NO MATCH is the expectation of record and it is what these blocks assert.
# pcrec answers (0,1) -- agreeing with python `re` and with a hand derivation of
# what a possessive quantifier is supposed to forbid, which is retreat into ITS
# OWN loop and not the freezing of a preceding `?`. U9's own entry classifies
# the divergence as PCRE2-SIDE ("all PCRE2-side, none analysis-side") and names
# THIS EVENT as the one that makes it reachable: "pcrec refuses the spelling
# today, module `atomic-groups`". [M6.4.2] is the day it stops refusing.
#
# SO THIS IS A RULING SOMEBODY OWES, not a bug [M6.4.2] chose to leave. The two
# available answers are "reproduce libpcre2 exactly, because D26 says so" and
# "keep the hand-derivation answer, because U9 is filed as libpcre2's own
# oddity" -- and the module lane is not the place to decide which. The ratchet
# is the honest holding place: it keeps the cells, it keeps them LOUD, and if
# pcrec is ever changed to reproduce U9 this file FIRES and says the deferral is
# over rather than letting the divergence become invisible.
#
# EVERY EXPECTATION BELOW IS libpcre2 10.46's, produced through the ctypes
# binding at docs/design/eng_brep_measurements/probes/pcre2_ctypes.py, and NOT
# what pcrec currently answers. That is the whole point of this directory.

pattern a?(?:b){0,4}+a
features atomic-groups
n "a"
ns 1 "a"
m "ba" 0 2
ns 1 "ba"
ns 2 "ba"

pattern (a?)(?>(b){0,4})a
features atomic-groups
n "a"
ns 1 "a"
